answer:
a. Identify the race condition(s):
number_of_processes is a shared variable updated with ++ and --, which are not atomic operations — each compiles to a read, a modify, and a write-back. If two processes call these routines concurrently (one allocating, one releasing, or two allocating at once), both can read the same old value before either writes back the new one, so one of the two updates is lost and the counter ends up incorrect.

A second, related race exists in the allocation routine itself: two threads can both evaluate the check number_of_processes == MAX_PROCESSES as false at the same time (because neither has incremented yet), and both proceed to allocate a process — so the system can end up creating more processes than MAX_PROCESSES allows, even though each individual thread's check looked safe.

b. Where to place the locking:
The acquire() must be taken before the if-check and only released after the increment (or after the decrement in the release routine), so the check-and-update happens as one atomic unit:

// allocate
mutex.acquire();
if (number_of_processes == MAX_PROCESSES) {
    mutex.release();
    return -1;
}
++number_of_processes;
mutex.release();

// release
mutex.acquire();
--number_of_processes;
mutex.release();

c. Would atomic_t number_of_processes be enough?
Only partially. Replacing the plain int with an atomic_t would make the increment and decrement themselves atomic, so the counter could never lose an update from ++/-- running concurrently. However, it would NOT fix the compound 'check-then-increment' race described above, because comparing number_of_processes to MAX_PROCESSES and then incrementing it are two separate operations — an atomic type only guarantees atomicity for a single operation, not for a sequence of them. Two threads could still both pass the == MAX_PROCESSES check before either increments, and the count could still exceed MAX_PROCESSES. A mutex protecting the whole check-and-increment sequence is still required.
